  <h2 id="money">The money filter: keep the fee below the whole budget</h2>

  <p>
    A degree is one line in the education budget. As a strict planning heuristic, keep the college fee near 10%
    of the total education budget.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the family's complete budget is Rs 10 lakh, that means about Rs 1 lakh for college and Rs 9 lakh for
    living security, a laptop, internet, books, projects, certifications and later upskilling. Treat spending
    above roughly 20% as a serious caution that needs course-specific proof. Both numbers are planning
    heuristics, not universal facts.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When paying more can make sense</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A government or convener seat at a strong institute where the median and placed share are verified.</li>
        <li>A licensed path where the degree is legally required, such as medicine, law or nursing.</li>
        <li>Scarce labs, clinical exposure or hospital training that you cannot rebuild yourself.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When it is likely waste</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A management-quota seat with no published median or denominator.</li>
        <li>An education loan for a path whose typical entry pay is Rs 2 to 3 lakh.</li>
        <li>A fee that leaves nothing for tools, internships and a portfolio.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Software, data analysis, design, writing and basic accounting can be learned through good YouTube channels,
    official documentation, open university courses and reputable low-cost courses. Check the instructor,
    the last update and whether the course makes you build something.
  </p>

  <p>
    A certificate is not proof; a finished project is. Medicine, law, nursing and pharmacy still need the
    formal degree and licence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="average">If your start is average: the plan that still pays</h2>

  <p>
    Most students will not reach the topper's median, and that is not a life sentence. Three moves matter more
    than the college name: pick one core skill, add one multiplier skill, and show one finished piece of proof.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">The 3 Gates: pass these before you commit money or a drop year.</p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      {gateItems.map((item) => (
        <div class="gate">
          <span class="gate__num">{item.name}</span>
          <span class="gate__name">{item.title}</span>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>A week that fits a student with limited time</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Three focused study blocks on the core skill, sized to your energy rather than a fixed count.</li>
    <li>One block to build or improve the proof piece.</li>
    <li>One short conversation with someone working in the field, asking what their first year actually paid.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The expensive wrong turns</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Paying a management-quota fee because a brochure quoted a package, without reading the placed share.</li>
    <li>Taking a loan for a path whose typical entry pay is smaller than the annual repayment.</li>
    <li>Preparing years for one government exam with no parallel skill.</li>
    <li>Choosing a path only because it appears near the top of a "high salary" list.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>If you are a parent</h3>

  <p>
    Ask the college for three numbers: how many graduated last year, how many were placed, and the median of
    those placed. Ask for the branch-wise median, not the college one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then ask your child what they can finish this month in the path. A placement report is not a promise, and
    a relative's story is not data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Pick two paths from the ledger, write down their cost, wait and median, and run each through the Cohort
    Test. Take one small real test of each, such as a mock paper, a mini project or a conversation with a working
    professional, for as long as it takes to get honest feedback.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some students need a few weeks, others a couple of months, and both are normal. If you want a structured
    plan built around your budget and your marks, choose
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous career
    guidance</a>. It builds a high-value skill portfolio around your chosen path, so your first income arrives
    earlier and compounds toward earlier financial freedom. If you would rather start smaller, try the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the general engines behind high pay, see the
    <a href="/blog/career-options/best-career-options-with-high-salary/">guide to what creates high
    salary</a>. For the map of every stream, see
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th/">career options after 12th</a>.
  </p>

